{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>This shall be the law of.</b> The entire verse is superfluous, for it should have said only ״כִּי יִטְהַר הַמְּצוֹרָע וְהוּבָא אֶל הַכֹּהֵן״ and so forth. And in Torat Kohanim they derived from the word ״זֹאת״ to exclude the purification of the metzora on a bamah, that it be only in the Eternal House, and the word ״תִּהְיֶה״ to include that metzora'im may be purified in this era — and like the deed of Rabbi Tarfon (Yerushalmi Pesachim 2:2; Torat Kohanim verse 4) who showed a staff with which he purified metzora'im in this era — and the word ״תּוֹרַת״ to say that although He distinguished among the tumah of the metzora, in that some are absolute upon the first sighting of the priest and some require a week's seclusion and so forth, nevertheless the law of the metzora is one. And the word ״בְּיוֹם״ is to say that the purification of the metzora is only by day and not by night. And they said: one might think even the taking of the birds and so forth applies by night — Scripture says ״זֹאת״, thus far." "Now, from the word ״זֹאת״ he derived to exclude the purification of the metzora on a bamah, and from it he derived to exclude the taking of the birds by day. The reason is that since it preceded the mention of purification and the mention of ״בְּיוֹם״, it excluded both of them — for if the intention of HaShem had been to exclude only one of them, He should have attached the exclusion to one in this manner: ״תּוֹרַת הַמְּצוֹרָע זֹאת תִּהְיֶה בְּיוֹם״, and then the exclusion would apply to ״בְּיוֹם״ and not to the law of the metzora, whether it requires the Eternal House. Or it may say in this manner: ״בְּיוֹם טַהֲרַת הַמְּצוֹרָע זֹאת תִּהְיֶה תּוֹרָתוֹ״, and then the exclusion would apply to the offerings and not to ״בְּיוֹם״, and in this arrangement there is no addition of even a single letter." "And in the Midrash they derived (Leviticus Rabbah 16:2) from the superfluity of the verse ״זֹאת תִּהְיֶה״ — this is the law of one who emits an evil name (motzi shem ra). And one needs to know why the verse alluded to this matter in this place, at the time of purification. And it appears that since the verse came to speak of the bringing of birds, therefore it prefaced by giving a reason: for since his sin is on account of evil speech, therefore this shall be his law — to bring birds that chirp, a model of himself." "<b>Further,</b> it appears to explain in this manner: although tzara'at, according to the nature of its constitution, develops from the darkening and corruption of the body and the predominance of bile that overpowers a person and makes an impression on his flesh — and this condition is brought on by anxiety and constriction of the heart and desolation of the mind — and the natural remedy for this is to remove the anxiety and things that broaden a person's heart and gladden him. Now, when a plague of tzara'at comes upon a person, a person might say that it is a natural illness that happens to a person, and when told that it is on account of evil speech, he will not believe and will not justify the matter. Therefore El Elyon devised wisely and commanded that the metzora be shut up — he shall dwell alone, his garments shall be torn, his head shall be disheveled, and he shall cover his upper lip — and these things, according to nature, are opposed to treating the cause of the plague, and moreover they will anew engender the plague. And when the person sees that even though he did these things that are opposed, nevertheless through reflecting on repentance and confessing his sin — for because of the iniquity that his tongue spread, the plague opened his eyes — and he turns from his sin and purifies his tongue and sees that the plague has opened his eyes, by this he will know and demonstrate a clear proof that the plague did not come upon him except because he spoke evil speech. And this is what the verse says: <b>this shall be the law of</b> one who emits an evil name, and the recognition and proof of the matter is <b>on the day of his purification</b>, for even though he did things opposed to his illness as mentioned, nevertheless he was purified — by this he will know that this is the law of his evil tongue and not a natural occurrence as the devisers of evil thought, and he will guard his mouth and his tongue. And this is what it concludes: <b>the plague of tzara'at is healed from the tzarua</b> — meaning, from his being a tzarua comes his healing, for he repented and it was healed for him." "<b>And he shall be brought, etc.</b> It was necessary to say <b>and he shall be brought</b> and so forth, for since it said ״בְּיוֹם טָהֳרָתוֹ״, one might have thought that a person would suppose the verse called him pure by the mere removal of his plague and there is no condition in the matter — therefore it said ״וְהוּבָא״ and so forth, meaning, both this and additionally: ״his purification and he shall be brought to״ and so forth. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Torat Kohanim) that he shall not delay. And the reason it said <b>and he shall be brought</b> and did not say ״and he came״, is perhaps that a court or the priest's authority compels him to come; and likewise what the verse said ״נֶגַע צָרַעַת וְגוֹ׳ וְהוּבָא אֶל הַכֹּהֵן״ is intended to say that they compel him to come if he is lazy."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :register {:flags {0 ["the Eternal"], 1 ["the Eternal"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:35.422121296Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}